export const START_HERE_LESSON_MD: string = "## Who this lesson is for\n\nIf you are new to AI, or have tried it a few times and wondered how to get more from it, start here. If you already use AI most days, some of these tips will be familiar. You can skim the headings and spend more time on \"Vital tips from your first month\".\n\nThe full course is for anyone from a complete beginner to a confident user. The first lessons make sure everyone has the same foundations. Later lessons move on to building tools, working with data and automating everyday work.\n\n## Why this lesson comes first\n\nMany of us began by typing long questions, reading every answer on screen, losing track of requests that worked and paying for subscriptions before we knew what each tool was good at.\n\nThese are habits we wish we had known at the start. Several take only a few minutes to set up. They can save you time in later lessons and help you remember what you learn.\n\nThis lesson has seven main tips, ten shorter habits and advice from the first month of the course. The shorter habits draw on research, guidance from AI companies and advice from experts. The project at the end helps you set up an AI starter kit for the rest of the course.\n\n## What you will be able to do by the end\n\n- Speak to AI instead of typing, and listen to long answers and lessons instead of reading them.\n- Ask AI plain questions and improve its answers by replying with what you need changed.\n- Ask AI to write a better request, or prompt, for you, without first learning special techniques for writing one.\n- Use examples to show AI what you want, instead of trying to describe it.\n- Keep a learning notebook for your notes, ideas and prompts that worked.\n- Start with free or low-cost plans and decide when paying more is worthwhile.\n- Use the GWTH labs to compare different versions of AI, called models, on the same real-life scenarios. See which model best fits your needs so you can save money when choosing one.\n\n## Tip 1: Speak to AI and listen when it helps\n\nWhen you have a lot to say, speaking may be quicker than typing. Most of us speak much faster than we type. OpenAI makes the same point in its guidance for people using ChatGPT at work.\n\nThere are three ways to speak to AI or listen to text:\n\n- **Dictation.** You speak and it types your words into the box. You can read and correct them before you press send.\n- **Voice mode.** You have a spoken conversation. You talk, and the AI talks back.\n- **Read aloud.** The AI, or your browser, reads an answer or a page to you.\n\n### Use dictation in any AI tool\n\nTry the dictation built into your computer or phone. It works in an AI tool's message box and in email.\n\n- **Windows:** press the Windows key and H together, then speak. It types into whatever box your cursor is in. It needs an internet connection.\n- **Mac:** turn on Dictation in System Settings, under Keyboard. Then press the microphone key, or use the dictation shortcut, and speak.\n- **iPhone and Android:** tap the microphone on the keyboard.\n\nChatGPT, Claude and Gemini also have their own microphone buttons in the message box. Check whether the button types into the message box or starts a spoken conversation.\n\nDictation can mishear names, place names and technical words. Read the text it produced before you send it, as you would check a text message.\n\nIn Month 1 of the course, you will build your own dictation tool with its own dictionary of your words. It gets better at recognising your voice the more you use it.\n\n:::tip\nYou do not need to speak in tidy sentences. Talk the way you would explain the problem to a colleague: \"I've got a letter from the council about a parking permit, I don't understand the second paragraph, can you explain it in plain English?\" AI copes well with the \"ums\" and second thoughts of normal speech.\n:::\n\n### Use voice mode to think out loud\n\nVoice mode lets you think something through, practise a difficult conversation or ask questions while you are walking or cooking. ChatGPT Voice, Claude's voice mode and Gemini Live in the Gemini phone app are available on free plans, with limits.\n\n### Listen instead of reading\n\nIf reading long answers or lessons is tiring, try listening to them.\n\n- In ChatGPT, use the speaker icon under a reply on the web, or press and hold a reply on your phone, to have it read aloud.\n- In Microsoft Edge, press Ctrl, Shift and U together to have any web page or PDF read aloud. You can choose the voice and the speed.\n- Google's NotebookLM can turn documents you upload into a spoken summary you can listen to like a podcast.\n\nEvery GWTH lesson has audio too, so you can listen while you walk if that suits you.\n\n## Tip 2: Ask AI as you would ask a new colleague\n\nYou can speak to AI in ordinary language. A prompt is the request you give it. According to the AI companies' own guidance, a clear goal is the main thing it needs.\n\nWhen you brief AI, give it the information you would give a capable new colleague. It cannot know what you mean by \"the usual format\" or what matters to your organisation unless you tell it. Anthropic, the company that makes Claude, suggests imagining how a colleague with no knowledge of the task would read your request. If that person would need more detail, add it for the AI too.\n\nWhen you ask AI for help:\n\n- **Use plain words.** \"Can you explain what a P60 is?\" is a good prompt.\n- **Give it the context a new starter would need.** Who is it for? What is it for? What does good look like?\n- **Include details you might take for granted.** If you ask for party food ideas, the AI cannot know that two of your guests are vegetarian unless you tell it.\n- **Reply to its answer.** Treat the first answer as a draft. Say what you want changed: \"Shorter, please.\" \"Make it warmer.\" \"My manager is new, so explain the acronyms.\"\n\nReplying to the first answer matters most. If it is bland, say what needs to change. You may get a better result after two or three replies.\n\nYou can also ask AI to show where it needs more information:\n\n- **Ask it to list its assumptions.** Before a bigger piece of work, ask: \"Before you start, list the assumptions you are making.\" Correct any that are wrong, then let it carry on.\n- **Ask anything you need to understand.** If an answer is too complicated, ask AI to explain it more plainly, as many times as you need. You can also ask how to use the tool, such as where to find a setting or what a word means. It may not know about features released in the last few months, so if its directions do not match what you see, check the tool's help pages.\n\nIf you return to the same saved chat, the AI can use its earlier messages. If you start a new chat, it may not have yesterday's details. Some tools have a memory feature that carries selected details between chats. Repeat anything important in your request. You also need to check the facts in its answers, as you will see below.\n\nLater in Month 1 you will learn a fuller way to brief AI for bigger jobs. For now, begin with plain questions and reply when an answer needs work.\n\n## Tip 3: Ask AI to write a prompt for you\n\nYou can ask AI to improve a prompt, including a prompt you plan to give that same AI. You do not have to learn special wording first. Anthropic and OpenAI both build tools for developers that do this, and OpenAI staff list \"ask it to improve your prompt\" among their everyday tips.\n\nTry one of these approaches.\n\n**Let it interview you.** Start with your rough request, then add:\n\n> Before you answer, ask me any questions you need to do this well. Ask them one at a time.\n\nThe AI will ask about your audience, your purpose and anything else it needs. Answering its questions is often quicker than trying to include every detail in your first request.\n\n**Let it improve your prompt.** Paste in what you were going to ask, then add:\n\n> Rewrite this as a better prompt. Then tell me what you changed and why.\n\nRead the explanation to see which details it added and why.\n\nWhen the result is good, copy the prompt into your learning notebook (Tip 5) so you can use it again.\n\n:::tip\nFor small jobs, the questions can be more trouble than they are worth. If the AI starts asking about something simple, say \"Skip the questions and just have a go.\" You can always correct it afterwards.\n:::\n\n## Tip 4: Show it an example whenever you can\n\nAn example shows AI what you mean by the format, tone or structure you want. Anthropic, the company that makes Claude, says examples are one of the most reliable ways to guide those parts of an answer.\n\n### Give an example of what you want\n\nIf you want an email, a report or a social media post in a particular style, paste in one you liked and say so:\n\n> Here is an email I wrote last month that went down well with my team. Write the new one about the office move in the same tone and at about the same length.\n\nOne example helps. Two or three examples give the AI a better sense of what they have in common, rather than leaving it to follow one too closely.\n\n### Give an example of what you do not want\n\nYou can also show an answer you do not want. Tell the AI what is wrong with it:\n\n> Here is the kind of answer I do not want. It is too formal and too long, and it uses jargon my readers will not know.\n\n### Share the document when you can\n\nIf you want the AI to use your company's style guide, your CV or a job advert, upload or paste the document after removing anything private. The document gives the AI details that your summary may miss.\n\n### Ask AI to give you an example\n\nWhen an explanation does not make sense, ask the AI for an example:\n\n> Can you give me an example of that from a job in HR in the UK?\n\nAn example from your own work may make the explanation clearer.\n\n:::tip\nWhen you find an example that gets great results, such as a model email or a well-written summary, save it in your learning notebook next to the prompt that used it. Good examples are worth keeping as much as good prompts.\n:::\n\n## Tip 5: Keep a learning notebook\n\nOver the next three months you will try many prompts, tools and ideas. Keep a notebook so you can find what worked and build on what you have learned.\n\n### What to keep\n\nGive your notebook these four headings:\n\n- **Prompts that worked.** Copy in the exact wording, any example you gave it, and a line about what it was for.\n- **What went wrong.** Wrong answers, made-up facts, tools that did not do what you hoped. These notes save you repeating a mistake.\n- **Ideas.** Things you would like to try with AI at work or at home.\n- **Questions.** Anything you want to come back to later in the course.\n\nYou can return to \"Prompts that worked\" whenever a similar task comes up.\n\n### Where to keep it\n\nYou can keep your notebook inside the course. On every GWTH lesson page, you can write notes next to the part you are reading. On the My notes page, you can find your notes from every lesson in one place.\n\nYour notes belong to you. You can download all of them at any time as a single file that Notion, Obsidian and most other notes apps can open. If you later choose another tool, you can take your notes with you.\n\nIf you would rather use your own tool from the start, choose one you will keep using:\n\n- **Notion.** Popular, flexible and free for personal use. A good choice if you already use it.\n- **Obsidian.** Free, including for work, and needs no account. Your notes are ordinary files saved on your own computer, so you control where they are kept.\n- **OneNote or a Word document.** Both work well, especially if your workplace already uses Microsoft 365.\n- **Joplin or Anytype.** Free options that keep your notes on your own device, if privacy matters most to you.\n\n### Keep related AI chats together\n\nYou can also keep related chats and files together inside some AI tools.\n\nMost AI tools have a **projects** feature that keeps chats, files and instructions for one piece of work together. You could set up a \"GWTH course\" project.\n\n**ChatGPT Space** is a different feature, launched by OpenAI in September 2026. It is a shared workspace where teams and AI work on documents together. At launch, it was available only on the higher-priced Pro plan and the business plans. You do not need it for this course.\n\nEven if you use a project in an AI tool, keep your learning notebook too. You can take your notes with you if you change tools. Writing things down in your own words also helps you remember them.\n\n## Tip 6: Start small and start cheap\n\nYou can begin learning on a free plan. The free plans of the main AI tools include chat, voice, web search and file uploads. Paid plans mainly let you use the tool more before you reach a limit. They also give you access to the strongest models, which are the most capable versions of the AI.\n\n### When to use a paid plan\n\n1. **Start free.** Use the free version of one or two tools for your first couple of weeks.\n2. **Consider one paid plan if you keep hitting limits** or need a feature your free plan does not include. Check important answers on any plan.\n3. **Pay for one plan at a time while you are learning.** Avoid paying for two subscriptions that do the same job.\n\nHere are the main plans. Always check the provider's own pricing page before you pay.\n\n| Tool | Free plan | Lower-cost plan | Main paid plan |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ChatGPT | Yes, with adverts in the UK | Go, under £10 a month, also with adverts | Plus, around £20 a month |\n| Claude | Yes | None | Pro, about £20 a month including VAT |\n| Google Gemini | Yes | Google AI Plus, £4.49 a month | Google AI Pro, £18.99 a month |\n| Meta AI | Yes, inside WhatsApp, Instagram and Facebook | None in the UK yet | None in the UK yet |\n| Grok | Yes, also inside X | SuperGrok Lite, £10 a month | SuperGrok, £30 a month |\n| Perplexity | Yes | None | Pro, £20 a month |\n| DeepSeek (China) | Yes, free | None | None |\n\nDeepSeek stores what you type in China, so keep anything personal or work-related out of it.\n\nIf your workplace gives you Microsoft Copilot, most of the tips in this lesson work there too.\n\n### Use smaller models for small jobs\n\nMost AI tools offer a faster, cheaper model alongside their most powerful one. For tidying an email, summarising a page or suggesting a subject line, try the faster model first. It is often good enough and uses less of your allowance. Try the most powerful model, or a \"think harder\" setting, when the stakes are high or the first answer is not good enough.\n\nEthan Mollick, a Wharton professor who writes widely about AI at work, says free models are fine when the stakes are low and paying for a good model is worthwhile once you use AI for real work. Let your own use guide when you pay.\n\nLater in Month 1 of the course, in a lesson called Your AI Garage, you will test free and low-cost models properly and learn how to keep your costs down as you use AI more.\n\n## Tip 7: Look at the labs before you choose\n\nClaims about the \"best\" AI model tell you little about how it will handle your own tasks. A model can do well at one task and poorly at a similar one. Researchers at Harvard and the Boston Consulting Group call this the \"jagged frontier\". The first full lesson in Month 1 of the course explains it in more detail.\n\nThe GWTH labs compare different AI models on the same real-life UK scenarios, such as writing a job advert or tidying a messy spreadsheet. Each lab shows the models' answers side by side, scores them against the same checklist and gives a dated verdict.\n\nYou can save money by choosing the model that best fits your needs. The labs show you which model that is.\n\nOpen the GWTH labs from the course page. Read two or three labs about tasks that interest you. For each one, ask:\n\n- Which answer would I rather send?\n- Which model asked better questions, or made fewer mistakes?\n- Which one sounds more like the way I write?\n\nNote your answers in your learning notebook to help you choose a model to start with. You can change tools later. In the course, you will also test tools on your own tasks.\n\n## Ten more habits worth starting now\n\n### 1. Use it daily on work you would rather put off\n\nEthan Mollick suggests about ten hours of real use before you can judge what AI is good for. The quickest way to get there is to use it for things you were going to do anyway. You might start with a tricky email, a report you have been putting off or a form you do not understand. Ten minutes a day adds up quickly.\n\n### 2. Check anything that matters\n\nAI can sound certain when it is wrong. It can invent facts, figures, quotations and even sources. In 2025, the High Court in London dealt with a case in which five legal cases cited in court did not exist. The judge said checking accuracy is the professional's responsibility.\n\nIn a Harvard and Boston Consulting Group study, consultants using AI finished more work, faster and to a higher standard on tasks it handled well. On a task it handled poorly, those using AI were less likely to get the right answer than those working without it.\n\nWhen an answer matters, take these steps:\n\n- **Ask for sources and open them.** Ask the AI to quote the part of your document it relied on.\n- **Tell it that \"I don't know\" is an acceptable answer.** Add a line such as \"If you are not sure, say so rather than guessing.\" This reduces made-up answers, although it does not stop them completely.\n- **Ask it to search the web for anything recent,** such as prices, new rules or this year's figures. What the AI knows on its own stops at a date in the past.\n\nFor anything with real consequences, such as health, money, the law or someone's job, check with a reliable source.\n\n### 3. Protect personal and confidential information\n\nDo not paste anything about a named person, or anything your employer would not want shared, into a personal AI account. That includes customers, patients, pupils, colleagues and family members. Screenshots and uploaded documents count too. At work, use the tools your organisation has approved and follow its AI policy.\n\nCheck your privacy settings too. In ChatGPT, Claude and Gemini you can choose whether your chats are used to train future models. Turning that off is a sensible default, but your chats may still be stored. Keep personal and confidential information out of a personal AI account.\n\n### 4. Ask for options, not one answer\n\nInstead of \"write a subject line\", try \"give me five subject lines in different tones\". Choosing from several options can be quicker than fixing one. It also helps you judge what works.\n\n### 5. Ask it to challenge you, not agree with you\n\nAI answers often agree with you, even when your idea needs a closer look. When you want a critical view, ask for one:\n\n- \"What are the three weakest points in this?\"\n- \"Argue against my plan. Where am I wrong?\"\n- \"What would a sceptical manager say about this?\"\n- \"What might I not be noticing?\"\n\nThis can give you a better second version and a clearer view of your own thinking.\n\n### 6. Rehearse a difficult conversation\n\nYou can practise a job interview, an appraisal or a conversation about a pay rise with AI. Describe the situation and ask it to play the other person. Practise your answers, ideally out loud in voice mode, then ask for feedback on how you came across. Describe the other person by their role, not by name.\n\n### 7. Break big jobs into steps\n\nFor a big piece of work, ask for an outline or plan first. Check it, then ask for one section at a time. You can check each section and correct the direction before the AI writes more.\n\n### 8. Make it yours before you send it\n\nNever send AI-written text exactly as it arrives. Read it, fill in any placeholders, such as [name] or [date], and change anything that does not sound like you. When you ask AI to proofread your own writing, ask it to show every change in bold so you can see what it changed.\n\n### 9. Save a short description of yourself\n\nMost AI tools let you save a short description of who you are, what you do and how you like answers written. In ChatGPT this is called custom instructions. A few lines such as \"I work in HR in the UK, I like short answers in plain British English\" save you repeating yourself in every chat. Check from time to time what the AI has saved about you in its memory, and remove anything you would rather it did not keep.\n\n### 10. Learn one thing at a time\n\nPick one AI tool and get to know it before adding another habit or feature. Some answers will be wrong, and some attempts will go nowhere. Use those attempts to learn what to ask or check next time.\n\n:::note\nYou may see advice online to offer AI a \"tip\", be very polite or even threaten it for better answers. A 2025 study from Wharton tested this across thousands of questions and found it made no reliable difference. Clear requests and good context work. Tricks do not.\n:::\n\n## Vital tips from your first month\n\nYou can try these tips now. The named lessons in Month 1 of the course give you more detail and practice.\n\n### Keep each chat focused\n\nThe context window is the amount of conversation the AI can use when it replies. It includes your messages and the AI's answers. In a long chat, important details can get buried and the answers can get worse.\n\nStart a new chat when you change topic. If a long chat starts going wrong, ask the AI to summarise the goal, the key facts and the decisions so far, then paste that summary into a fresh chat. You will learn why this works in Month 1 of the course, in a lesson called How AI Works.\n\n### Share the relevant part of a document\n\nAI tends to pay more attention to the start and end of a long document than to the middle. Researchers call this \"lost in the middle\". If only two paragraphs of a four-page letter matter, paste those two paragraphs and put your question at the start or end. You will try this in Month 1 of the course, in a lesson called How AI Works.\n\n### Know the difference between a subscription and an API\n\nThere are two main ways to pay for AI. A subscription, such as ChatGPT Plus or Claude Pro, is a fixed monthly price for using the app, with limits on how much you can use it. An API lets developers connect AI to their own software. It is charged by how much text the AI reads and writes.\n\nYou can learn on a free plan. If you later need to pay, an app subscription is usually easier to budget for than API use. You pay for the API separately: ChatGPT Plus does not cover it. In Month 1 of the course, a lesson called Your AI Garage compares the two and shows how to keep costs down as you use AI more. The course explains in detail when to use a subscription and when to use an API.\n\nThis may change. Many people expect subscription prices to rise if OpenAI and Anthropic list on the stock market.\n\n### Give AI a fuller brief for bigger jobs\n\nFor anything more than a quick question, a good brief covers five things: the role you want the AI to take, the task, the context, the format you want back and any limits, such as length or reading level. You will practise this in Month 1 of the course, in a lesson called Your AI Colleague.\n\n### Use a thirty-second fact check\n\nAsk for sources, open one or two and check the names, numbers and dates that matter. This quick routine catches many common mistakes. In Month 1 of the course, a lesson called How AI Works gives you a thirty-second checklist.\n\n### Check the main AI tools once a month\n\nAI tools change every few weeks, and today's leader may not lead in three months. Spend one minute each month checking the main AI companies' pricing and news pages for changes. In Month 1 of the course, a lesson called Frontier Labs Tooling shows you how and helps you choose the one plan that suits you.\n\n### Stay in control when AI takes actions\n\nAI tools called agents can send emails, fill in forms and browse websites on your behalf. They are useful, but they bring risks, such as following instructions hidden in a web page or email. In Month 1 of the course, a lesson called Agent Safety gives you five rules for staying in control.\n\n## Getting the most from the course\n\nAs you work through the course:\n\n- **Set a regular time.** Choose a daily slot that you can keep more easily than a big block once a week.\n- **Read or listen, then do the project.** Each lesson has a project. Doing it helps you remember what you learned.\n- **Use AI to help you learn.** If a lesson explains something in a way that does not click, ask an AI to explain it differently, or to give you an example from your own job.\n- **Answer the questions as you go.** Every lesson already has short questions spaced through it. They help you check that you remember each part as you go, rather than facing a long quiz at the end. They are straightforward and are not there to trick you.\n- **Keep your notebook open.** Add notes as you go, while the details are fresh.\n- **Come back to the labs.** The GWTH labs compare different AI models on the same real-life scenarios and show how each one does. They show which model best fits your needs, so you can save money when choosing one. New labs are added as tools change, so your choice of model can change too.\n\n## Your project: set up your AI starter kit\n\nSet aside about 25 minutes to prepare these five things for the rest of the course. The project for this lesson gives you the full steps:\n\n1. Turn on dictation on your computer or phone and use it to ask an AI one real question.\n2. Create your learning notebook with the four headings from Tip 5.\n3. Ask an AI to interview you about a real task. After you answer its questions, ask it to write a reusable prompt for that task. Save the prompt in your notebook.\n4. Check your privacy setting in the AI tool you use most.\n5. Look at one GWTH lab and note which answer you preferred and why.\n\n## Recap\n\n- Speak instead of typing, and listen instead of reading, when it helps you.\n- Ask AI as you would ask a new colleague: use plain questions, give it context and reply to improve the answer.\n- Let AI interview you or rewrite your prompt, then save the prompts that work.\n- Show AI examples of what you want, and of what you do not want.\n- Keep a learning notebook so you can find your notes, ideas and useful prompts again.\n- Start free, pay for one plan when it is worth it, and use smaller models for small jobs.\n- Look at the labs to see how different models handle the same real-life scenarios, then test them yourself later.\n- Use AI daily on tasks you tend to put off. Check important answers and protect personal information. Ask for options and a critical view when you need them.\n- Rehearse difficult conversations, break big jobs into steps, make the text yours before you send it, and learn one thing at a time.\n- Keep each chat focused, share the relevant part of a document and know how a subscription differs from the API.\n\n## Next: Welcome to GWTH\n\nIn Month 1 of the course, in a lesson called Welcome to GWTH, you will meet the six ways AI can help you. You will also see what you will be able to build over the three months. Keep your notebook open to add what you learn.\n"

export const START_HERE_PROJECT_MD: string = "# Student Project: Your AI Starter Kit With a Voice Key\n\n## Why this matters\n\nIn about 25 minutes, you'll set up three things you'll use in every lesson that follows. The first is a voice key: a keyboard shortcut that types what you say into any box on your computer. The second is a short set of saved instructions that tells your AI tool how you like your answers. The third is a learning notebook for the prompts that work for you. A prompt is the instruction or question you give an AI.\n\nThese turn three of the tips in this lesson into habits that are already set up, rather than tips you have to remember.\n\n## Before you start: protect private information\n\n- Use a personal task for this project, or a work task that contains no personal or confidential information.\n- Do not paste anything about a named person, such as a customer, patient, pupil, colleague or family member, into a personal AI account.\n- If you want to try this with a work task, use the AI tool your organisation has approved. Follow its policy.\n\nIf you would not post it on a public noticeboard, do not paste it into a personal AI account.\n\n## What you need\n\n- A Windows computer or a Mac with a microphone. A phone or tablet works too, using the route at the end of Step 1.\n- A free account with at least one AI tool, such as ChatGPT, Claude or Gemini.\n- About 25 minutes.\n\n## Step 1: Set up your voice key with Handy (8 minutes)\n\nHandy is a free dictation app for Windows and Mac. You press a shortcut, speak, and it types your words into whatever box your cursor is in: an AI chat, an email or a document. It turns your speech into text on your own computer, so your voice is not sent to the internet.\n\n1. Go to [handy.computer](https://handy.computer) and download the version for your computer. Install it as you would any other app.\n2. Open Handy. It will ask to download a speech model, which is the part that recognises your words. Choose the one it suggests. The download is several hundred megabytes, so it may take a few minutes.\n3. On a Mac, Handy will also ask for permission to use the microphone and for Accessibility access, which lets it type into other apps. Allow both. If you miss a request, open System Settings, then Privacy & Security, and turn Handy on under Microphone and under Accessibility.\n4. Open your AI tool and click in the message box.\n5. Hold down **Ctrl and Space** together on Windows, or **Option and Space** together on a Mac. Keep holding the keys while you ask one real question out loud about something you'd like to know this week. For example: \"I've been asked to give a five-minute talk at work about a project I finished. Can you suggest a simple structure?\"\n6. Release the keys to stop recording. If Handy keeps recording after you release them, press the shortcut again to stop. You can change the shortcut in Handy's settings. You can also choose whether to hold the keys or press once to start and again to stop.\n7. Read what Handy typed. Correct any mistakes, then press send.\n\n**If you cannot install apps on this computer,** for example on a work laptop, use the dictation already built in:\n\n- **Windows:** press the Windows key and H together.\n- **Mac:** go to System Settings, then Keyboard, and turn on Dictation. Use the shortcut shown there.\n- **iPhone or Android:** tap the microphone on the keyboard.\n\n**Listen to the answer too.** Look for a Read aloud or speaker option beside the reply. In ChatGPT, look below the reply for a speaker icon, or open the three-dot menu and choose Read aloud. If your AI tool does not have one, open the chat in Microsoft Edge and choose Read aloud from its menu, or press Ctrl, Shift and U.\n\n**Check:** you asked a question without typing it.\n\n## Step 2: Save how you like your answers (5 minutes)\n\nMost AI tools let you save instructions that they follow in every new chat, so you don't have to repeat them.\n\n1. Find the box for saved instructions in the AI tool you use most:\n   - **ChatGPT:** Settings, then Personalization, then Custom instructions.\n   - **Claude:** click your initials, then Settings, then Your Claude. Enter your preferences under Instructions. In the older layout, look under Account for Instructions for Claude.\n   - **Gemini:** Settings, then Personal context, then Your instructions for Gemini.\n\n   Menus change. If you cannot find the setting, check your tool's help page for saved instructions. Some accounts do not offer this feature. If yours does not, save the instructions in your learning notebook in Step 3. Paste them at the start of each new chat, then ask your question.\n\n2. Use your voice key to say these instructions into the box, or type them. If your account has no saved-instructions setting, enter them at the start of your chat instead. Change any line so that it suits you:\n\n   > Give me the answer first, in plain English, then the detail. Keep answers short unless I ask for more. If you are not sure about something, say so. Ask me a question if my request is unclear.\n\n3. Save the instructions if your tool offers that setting. Start a new chat and ask your question from Step 1 again. If you cannot save instructions in your tool, paste them before your question. Compare the new answer with the first one.\n\n**Check:** you have used your instructions in a new chat and compared the answers.\n\n## Step 3: Create your learning notebook (5 minutes)\n\n1. Choose where to keep your notes. You can use the notes on this lesson page. You can download all your course notes later. You can also use Notion, Obsidian, OneNote, a Word document or another tool you already like.\n2. If you are using your own tool, create a page or document called **GWTH learning notebook**.\n3. Add these four headings:\n   - Prompts that worked\n   - What went wrong\n   - Ideas\n   - Questions\n4. Under **Prompts that worked**, paste the instructions you used in Step 2, so you can reuse them in another AI tool or paste them at the start of each new chat.\n5. Under **Ideas**, write one thing you'd like AI to help you with by the end of the course. Try saying it with your voice key.\n\n**Check:** your notebook has all four headings, your saved instructions and at least one idea.\n\n## Step 4: Let AI write a prompt with you (5 minutes)\n\n1. Choose a real task you will do in the next week, such as an email, a short plan or a summary of something you have read.\n2. Use your voice key to describe the task to your AI tool in a sentence or two. Then add:\n\n   > Before you answer, ask me any questions you need to do this well. Ask them one at a time.\n\n3. Answer its questions. Speaking is quicker than typing here.\n4. When it has done the task, ask:\n\n   > Write a prompt for a similar task next time. Leave clear spaces for the details I will need to change.\n\n5. Copy that prompt into your notebook under **Prompts that worked**. Add one line explaining what the prompt is for.\n\n**Check:** you have saved one prompt that you could reuse next week.\n\n## Step 5: Check your privacy setting (2 minutes)\n\nIf you only use an account provided by your organisation, check its policy on how chats are used. Record what you find in your notebook.\n\nIf you use a personal AI account, find the setting that controls whether your chats are used to train future models:\n\n- **ChatGPT:** Settings, then Data controls.\n- **Claude:** Settings, then Privacy.\n- **Gemini:** your Gemini Apps Activity settings.\n\nIf you cannot find the setting, ask the AI: \"Where do I find the setting that controls whether my chats are used for training?\" Decide whether to leave it on or turn it off, and note your choice under **Questions** in your notebook.\n\n**Check:** you have recorded your setting choice or what you found about your organisation's policy.\n\n## What you have prepared\n\n- A voice key that types what you say into any box on your computer.\n- Instructions saved in your AI tool or your notebook, ready to use in each new chat.\n- A learning notebook with four headings and your first saved prompt.\n- A privacy setting you have checked and chosen, or notes on your organisation's policy.\n\nIf you have five more minutes, open the GWTH Labs page from the course menu and read one lab that compares two AI answers to a task like yours. Later in Month 1, you'll go further and build your own private speech-to-text tool.\n\nKeep the notebook open in the next lesson and add to it as you go.\n"
